# Commit after every change

After each completed change, create a git commit before starting the next change.

- Stage only files that belong to that change.
- Write a short message that states why the change was made.
- Do not commit secrets (`.env`, credentials, keys).
- Do not push unless the user asks.
- Do not amend a pushed commit. If a hook rejects the commit, fix the problem and create a new commit.
- If there is nothing to commit, do not create an empty commit.

```bash
git add path/to/files
git commit -m "Explain why this change exists."
```
